Dear Laura,

Solidon3D 0.2.2 has been released. The relevant TCT angle is a local desktop
workflow that keeps imported-geometry edits, print-oriented features and
material assignment connected through a non-destructive operation history.

The release can resize a recognised hole in imported STL or 3MF geometry while
preserving its original location and axis. The edit remains parameterised;
standards-based inserts, fastener seats and bearing seats can then use fit
values measured from a printed calibration artefact.

For the handoff, each physical filament spool can carry temperatures, cooling,
retraction and material properties. Solidon writes those values into the
correct 3MF material position and maps them to the external slicer, instead of
treating colour and process data as unrelated selections.

This is desktop polymer-AM preparation rather than enterprise PLM or a slicer.
Projects stay local, analysis values remain distinct from imported G-code
metrics, and every change is a reproducible operation. An optional language
model may call the same fixed API as the UI, but cannot calculate geometry or
execute generated code.

Solidon runs on Windows, macOS and Linux without an account, telemetry or cloud
project store: https://solidon3d.de/en/

I can provide a compact technical briefing, the released 0.2.2 build, a sample
STL-to-3MF workflow and publication-ready imagery.

